content.text:
{
  "name": "JSRunner",
  "params": {
    "jsPath": "js/smAgent.js",
    "jobParams": {
      "repo": "epam/dmtools-dart",
      "maxTriggeredWorkflows": 3,
      "validationConcurrency": 3,
      "validationConcurrencyQueueWatermark": 3,
      "rules": [
        {
          "description": "RULE ORDER (dmtools-agents#682, live fa#1202/#1203 2026-10-03 23:05–23:57, still reproducing 2026-10-04 06:21–06:22): runs FIRST, ahead of merge-validated AND sweep-stale-validating (whose relative order is a documented #637 invariant — this rule preserves it by prefixing, never interleaving). A cancelled required check is never a verdict: the sweep skips cancelled conclusions by design and fail-validation owns red, so this rule cannot fight them — it never touches labels and never dispatches workflows, it only re-runs GitHub Actions runs. GitHub PR: armed (ai_validating) or latched (ai_validated) head whose REQUIRED check contexts (rule.requiredContexts, branch protection here: static/gate/agents-gate; in fa the kicker's sm-liveness/head-completeness) concluded CANCELLED — the kicker cancel-loop death: every silent-refresh push queued a new kicker run and the pending one was superseded (cancelled) in the shared concurrency group, required contexts died CANCELLED on fresh heads, PRs BLOCKED until manual reruns (2 dead zones + 2 manual reruns on 2026-10-03 alone). CANCELLED is read with branch-protection semantics: the LATEST check run per context is the verdict — a stale cancel under a fresh green re-stamp does NOT act (#695 review fix). NOT failure — failure paths exist; NOT pending-with-active-runs — the localAction probes every run on the head (hasActiveDispatchedRun pattern widened to all events) and skips while anything is in flight. localAction rerun_cancelled_checks re-runs (gh run rerun) the latest attempt-1 cancelled run per required context and posts a once-per-head marker comment citing the evidence — ONLY when the rerun set covers every cancelled context (a partial set defers the marker so the uncovered contexts stay actionable; #695). BEHIND (silent-update refreshes the head) and DIRTY (conflict-rework owns it) are excluded, and agent:rework defers to the rework leg. limit 2: the observed death takes two PRs per wave (fa#1202+#1203 in one 36-second push wave). gh-755 SPLIT (owner directive 2026-10-05 'cancelled must re-run'): machine-authored armed heads moved to rerun-any-cancelled-checks — the ANY-cancelled sibling DIRECTLY BELOW, whose rerun set is a superset of this rule's (a cancelled required context is included in 'any'); this rule keeps the required contexts for GUEST heads only (query.notMachine — the twins are author-disjoint, exactly one rerun rule matches any PR, no double rerun, no marker interference; with no machineAuthor configured notMachine is inert and this rule again covers every PR, so the coverage degrades to the pre-gh-755 behavior, never below it). Guests keep the NAMED-context scope deliberately: re-running the deployment's own branch protection on a guest head is the machine's business, a guest's foreign CI is not.",
          "source": "github",
          "query": {
            "type": "pr",
            "labels": [
              "ai_validating",
              "ai_validated"
            ],
            "notLabels": [
              "agent:rework"
            ],
            "notMergeState": [
              "BEHIND",
              "DIRTY"
            ],
            "notMachine": true,
            "draft": false
          },
          "localAction": "rerun_cancelled_checks",
          "requiredContexts": [
            "static",
            "gate",
            "agents-gate"
          ],
          "limit": 2,
          "id": "rerun-cancelled-checks"
        },
        {
          "description": "gh-755 (owner directive 2026-10-05: 'если что-то канселится — надо повторно запускать' — a CANCELLED run is not a verdict; rework legs on cancellations burn tokens for nothing): the ANY-cancelled twin of rerun-cancelled-checks, sitting IMMEDIATELY after it (the #682 rule-order invariant stands: rerun-cancelled-checks stays FIRST; the #637 invariant is untouched — this prefix never interleaves merge-validated / sweep-stale-validating). Live gap (2026-10-05, dmtools-agents PRs #752/#753): the kicker's sm-liveness + docs-freshness push runs ended cancelled/failed after the 15m verdict window — the named rule only re-runs the deployment's branch-protection requiredContexts, so every OTHER workflow's cancelled context (kicker sm-liveness / head-completeness, docs-freshness, CodeQL, …) had NO remedy: the head stayed BLOCKED/UNSTABLE, the merge stalled, and the fail-validation legs burned rework on the rollup mess. This rule re-runs ANY check context whose LATEST check run on the head reads CANCELLED (rule.anyCancelled — no name list; the localAction scans the whole check-runs rollup). Branch-protection semantics preserved (#695): the LATEST run per context is the verdict — a stale cancel under a fresh green re-stamp never acts, a fresh cancel over a stale green does. MACHINE-AUTHOR-ONLY (query.prMachineAuthor, fails closed when no machineAuthor is configured — in that deployment this rule is inert and the named rule covers everyone): a guest head's non-required cancels are the guest's own CI, not ours to re-run; the machine re-runs only its own heads. notMachine on the named rule makes the twins author-disjoint — exactly one rerun rule matches any PR, so the once-per-head markers never interfere and no run is ever rerun twice. Same guards as the named rule: the localAction probes every run on the head (hasActiveHeadRun, all events) and skips while anything is in flight (an in-flight run re-stamps the contexts itself); rerun targets are the newest attempt-1 cancelled run per cancelled context (run_attempt > 1 already fired the remedy — no loop); a partial coverage set defers the once-per-head marker so uncovered contexts stay actionable (#695); BEHIND (silent-update refreshes the head) and DIRTY (conflict-rework owns it) are excluded; agent:rework in notLabels defers to the rework leg. FAIL-VALIDATION PARITY (gh-755 ask 2): the fail path's arm-time red-verdict probe (headHasRealFailure) treats a cancelled-only rollup as no-verdict — a PR whose only red is a cancelled check gets a rerun, never an agent:rework arm. limit 2: parity with the named rule (the observed death takes two PRs per wave).",
          "source": "github",
          "query": {
            "type": "pr",
            "labels": [
              "ai_validating",
              "ai_validated"
            ],
            "notLabels": [
              "agent:rework"
            ],
            "notMergeState": [
              "BEHIND",
              "DIRTY"
            ],
            "prMachineAuthor": true,
            "draft": false
          },
          "localAction": "rerun_cancelled_checks",
          "anyCancelled": true,
          "limit": 2,
          "id": "rerun-any-cancelled-checks"
        },
        {
          "id": "reconcile-rework-vs-approval",
          "description": "gh-807 VERDICT RECONCILIATION, shape 1 of 3 (live fa PR #1428, 2026-10-09 05:47:30Z + 05:47:40Z): two review legs concluded on the SAME head 97s apart with contradictory verdicts and both side effects stuck — pr_approved + agent:rework coexisting; the tick evaluated labels without ever reading the verdicts. Review legs stamp a machine-parseable verdict record on the PR (js/common/reviewVerdicts.js marker comment: head sha + effective verdict + thread census; free-form comment text is never consulted — AC4). CONFLICT-SHAPED QUERIES (gh-807 review): the original single rule (labels [pr_approved, agent:rework] + latestVerdict [...] ) matched EVERY sticky-approved PR forever — each tick paid github_get_pr + github_get_pr_comments + github_get_issue per approved PR just to log 'already agree'. The three shapes below match only actual contradictions, shrinking the standing match set to ~zero; the reconcile_verdicts localAction is unchanged (verdict-driven, idempotent). This shape: a PR-carrier agent:rework (rework-unresolved-threads / manual rework-on-label) whose newest verdict is APPROVE — the arm is review-driven and stale, the label comes off the PR (and the linked issue — the action reconciles BOTH carriers with one comment citing both verdict sources, AC2). The action probes the head's CI verdict before stripping (headHasRealFailure): a REAL red keeps the arm — red-CI rework on a sticky-approved PR (fail_validation) and the sticky dead-letter issue arm are CI-driven, not review losers; an unreadable/empty rollup fails open the same way (DIRTY conflict-rework arms survive, no strip/re-add yo-yo); and the record's OWN blocking census keeps it too — blocking > 0 is exactly the evidence AC3's blocking-threads exception arms on (rework-unresolved-threads grants that arm BECAUSE of the census), so it is evidence-driven even on a green head (stripping it would cost a tick of dispatch delay plus a ⚖️ comment claiming 'suggestions do not justify a rework arm' while the record says the findings were BLOCKING). Clean rollback: only machine-owned label carriers reconcile; ruleset approvals and human review state are untouched, and PRs without verdict records fail open (pre-gh-807 behavior). Runs before the merge window every tick, after the rerun twins (#682/#637 invariants untouched). limit 10: pure label writes + one comment — waves reconcile in one tick.",
          "source": "github",
          "query": {
            "type": "pr",
            "labels": [
              "agent:rework"
            ],
            "latestVerdict": [
              "APPROVE"
            ],
            "draft": false
          },
          "localAction": "reconcile_verdicts",
          "limit": 10
        },
        {
          "id": "reconcile-issue-rework-vs-approval",
          "description": "gh-807 VERDICT RECONCILIATION, shape 2 of 3 — the issue-carrier twin of reconcile-rework-vs-approval: review CHANGES_REQUESTED (markForSmStoryRework), red-CI (fail_validation) and conflict-rework (DIRTY) arms all land on the LINKED ISSUE, which pr-type label queries cannot see — without this shape an issue-carrier arm stranded next to an APPROVE verdict (the #1428 issue-carrier variant: the RC leg armed the issue, the APPROVE leg won 97s later) would never reconcile. Conflict shape only: an issue whose newest verdict for the linked PR's CURRENT head is APPROVE while the issue still carries agent:rework (the verdict guard reads the linked PR head via the issue enrichment). The reconcile_verdicts action strips the loser from BOTH carriers with one comment citing both verdict sources (AC2) — subject to the same arm-preserving exemptions (headHasRealFailure: a real red keeps the arm, so fail_validation re-arms and rework-on-red-ci dead-letter recovery stay intact; and the winning APPROVE record's blocking>0 census — the evidence AC3's blocking-threads exception arms on). Runs before rework-on-red-ci in rule order: a stale arm is stripped before the dispatch rule can fire it; a CI-driven arm survives and dispatches normally. PRs without verdict records fail open.",
          "source": "github",
          "query": {
            "type": "issue",
            "labels": [
              "agent:rework"
            ],
            "prState": "OPEN",
            "latestVerdict": [
              "APPROVE"
            ]
          },
          "localAction": "reconcile_verdicts",
          "limit": 10
        },
        {
          "id": "reconcile-approval-vs-changes",
          "description": "gh-807 VERDICT RECONCILIATION, shape 3 of 3: a sticky pr_approved whose newest verdict for the CURRENT head is REQUEST_CHANGES/BLOCK — the approval is stale, approval-gated paths are blocked until re-review, and the label comes off the PR (pr_approved never lives on the issue; the action still posts the one both-sources comment, AC2). NOT gated on the arm-preserving exemptions (a checksRed red or a blocking>0 census keep agent:rework, never the approval): red CI never legitimizes an approval. Runs before the merge window every tick so merge-validated/validate-armed (which also carry notLatestVerdict as belt-and-suspenders for the same tick) see reconciled labels; the notLatestVerdict lanes make this rule's work mostly convergent the moment it fires. PRs without verdict records fail open (pre-gh-807 behavior).",
          "source": "github",
          "query": {
            "type": "pr",
            "labels": [
              "pr_approved"
            ],
            "latestVerdict": [
              "REQUEST_CHANGES",
              "BLOCK"
            ],
            "draft": false
          },
          "localAction": "reconcile_verdicts",
          "limit": 10
        },
        {
          "description": "Validated-but-stale: armed (ai_validating) + validation concluded but BASE MOVED (BEHIND only). BLOCKED must NOT unarm: since #483 BLOCKED is a real verdict, and on a freshly armed head BLOCKED simply means 'required checks pending' — unarming there races validate-armed in the SAME tick and deadlocks the PR forever (live: fa pr-762 armed at 08:25:37, unarmed 08:25:58 by the BLOCKED match, re-armed next tick, loop). silent-update-behind excludes ai_validating and merge-validated needs CLEAN, so without this unarm a genuinely moved base still deadlocks (live: fa pr-744) — BEHIND-only keeps that recovery and stops the self-inflicted one. gh-751 VERDICT-AWARE STRIP (live: fa #1295 — the arm was stripped FIVE times in one day with zero latches, [ai_validating]→[]→[ai_validating] yo-yo under a saturated CI queue): every main merge made the armed head BEHIND and this strip DISCARDED the head's ALREADY-CONCLUDED green verdict — it lost the race to the strip (merge-validated needs CLEAN; validated-green sits later and excludes pr_approved), silent-update refreshed, validate-fresh re-armed, the next merge re-struck: a full re-validation per main merge. The action now probes the head's dispatched validation runs before stripping: a GREEN run ANYWHERE on the head latches ai_validated instead of the bare unarm — not only the newest run: gh-748 showed two workflow_dispatch runs landing on ONE head seconds apart, and the latch consumer (validate-armed's skipIfValidatedHead → hasSuccessfulDispatchedRun) accepts any completed-green run on the head, so the strip consumes that same facet (complete_validation parity — sweep_stale_validation's success-branch shape; the latch excludes the PR from validate-fresh, and validate-armed latch-skips on the unchanged head so the merge window re-flows without new CI); only when NO green run exists on the head (red / absent / in-flight / cancelled-only) does the bare unarm stand (refresh + re-validate recovery).",
          "source": "github",
          "query": {
            "type": "pr",
            "labels": [
              "ai_validating"
            ],
            "mergeState": [
              "BEHIND"
            ],
            "draft": false
          },
          "localAction": "unarm_validation",
          "limit": 1,
          "id": "unarm-stale-validation"
        },
        {
