  "description": "Auto rework leg: agent:rework armed on the issue either by the review verdict (CHANGES_REQUESTED) or by red pre-merge CI. Pushing fixes removes the label (pr_rework removeLabels). prMachineAuthor: auto rework fires ONLY on machine-authored PRs (owner rule) — the machine login is deployment config (jobParams.machineAuthor / config.machineAuthor / factory-sm machine-author input), never hardcoded; unconfigured fails closed. Manual rework on ANY PR: label the PR agent:rework (see rework-on-label). workflowRef={branch}: the leg runs on the PR head — GitHub links the run to the PR (visible in PR checks/timeline as the review/rework run). gh-715 ONE-WINS (live fa gh-1226 2026-10-04 18:47:11/13 — runs 37225765526/37225768365, same head SHA 23dacd10, both dispatched 2s apart by ONE tick): agent:rework can sit on BOTH carriers at once — the issue (this rule) AND the PR (armed by rework-unresolved-threads; gh-757 removed the guest validation-red arm — auto-rework is machine-only, so the PR carrier is machine-authored by construction now) — and each carrier's in-flight guard keys on its own anchor token ('· gh-N' vs '· pr-N'), so both dispatch rules fired for the same work. notPrLabels agent:rework makes this rule defer to the PR-carrier rule whenever the PR is armed: rework-on-label is PR-anchored (the #544-safe anchor), consumes its label on dispatch, and the engine's cross-anchor guard (smAgent triggerWorkflow) suppresses a dispatch while a leg for the SAME issue/PR is already in flight under the other anchor — exactly one leg wins per arm event. No consumeLabels here by design: the issue arm must survive dispatch so a failed/never-started leg is re-fired (the dead-letter recovery the machine loop is built on); the issue-anchored leg itself clears the label via pr_rework removeLabels.",
